#!/bin/sh /etc/rc.common
#
# Put back the add-ons this gateway wants, after a boot that lost them.
#
# A firmware upgrade replaces the overlay's packages with the new image's, so
# an add-on installed on the old firmware is gone after it, and a rollback
# loses one installed since. What was wanted survives in UCI, and
# `pulzr-addon restore` installs it again from the release now running.
#
# Never before the firmware is judged. On the first boot after an upgrade the
# state is pending and the health gate (98) is deciding whether to keep this
# firmware; an add-on installed then could be what fails it, and is the wrong
# thing to spend a metered link on before the answer. pulzr-firmware confirm
# starts the restore itself once the gate passes. Every other boot, including
# the one after a rollback, starts it here.
#
# Except for what the upgrade carried: an add-on that may be the backhaul
# (pulzr-cellular on a USB modem) was fetched for this release before the
# upgrade, and goes back on this first boot, from that copy, so the gate has a
# backhaul to judge. `restore --carried` also deletes a copy for any other
# release. See pulzr-addon, "CARRIED ACROSS AN UPGRADE".
#
# START=99, after everything an install's own health gate judges. Backgrounded:
# a restore downloads, and retries with backoff while it cannot (pulzr-addon).
# Almost every boot finds nothing missing and it exits at once.

START=99

start() {
    state="$(/usr/sbin/pulzr-firmware status 2>/dev/null | jsonfilter -e '@.state' 2>/dev/null)"
    if [ "${state}" = "pending" ]; then
        ( /usr/sbin/pulzr-addon restore --carried >/dev/null 2>&1 & )
        return 0
    fi
    ( { /usr/sbin/pulzr-addon restore --carried; /usr/sbin/pulzr-addon restore --retry; } >/dev/null 2>&1 & )
}
